A||||19||Scott_M||Smog Failure...HI NOx||||1||
Z||000000||Scott_M||11-14-2000||07:45 PM||||Well i took the old girl to get SMOG tested and she failed. The Nox readings were 636 ppm over the Max @15 mpg, and 531 ppm over the Max @25mph. If I understand it correctly, NOx is based on the temp of the engine, right? Well, I haven't replaced the plugs in about a year, and when I drained and refilled the radiator, I may have put a smidge more water that coolant. Could that have coused the high reading? Or do I just need a new EGR valve? Any advise would be appreciated. ||||reg||1
Z||000001||theogre||11-14-2000||07:50 PM||||unless they test under conditions that open the EGR....... most likely the catalyst wasn't hot enough or is bad.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Scott_M||11-14-2000||07:58 PM||||The cat I had replaced in 97'. The car had been sitting for a few days until I drove it straight to the test center. Also, they have a roller that is supposed to simulate road conditions at 15 and 25 mph.||||reg||1
Z||000003||zeusrekning||11-14-2000||08:32 PM||||Man the smog stuff sounds strict.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||Scott_M||11-14-2000||09:14 PM||||California has the strictest SMOG guidelines in the country I believe. ||||reg||1
Z||000005||88formula||11-14-2000||09:25 PM||||The two most common things that cause your NOX emissions to become high are over advanced timing and an inoperative EGR valve. If your engine is not overheating than its not the coolant mix.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000006||strdream||11-14-2000||10:15 PM||||The hot they refer to is the combustion chamber and exhaust temp. Caused by a lean burn. EGR you might say artificially richens the mixture by displacing oxygen. I would also look at the oxygen sensor. As they age they get slow or lazy. My not be bad to run a can of injector cleaner thru the gas tunk. But do it BEFORE you replace the oxygen sensor. There are some good ones and some not so good. Use one that actualy works. Anyone know of a good one that is availible in LA? <P>Water cools better than antifreeze. Why do you think they have you mix it 50/50 with water. you don't want to run pure water for the corrosion  protection, anti clog, anti boil and electrolite buffers, Etc you get with antifreeze.   <P>Ross||||reg||1
Z||000007||theogre||11-14-2000||10:38 PM||||The fact you let it sit could be enough. drive the hell out of it for an hour or so and test again.<P>I don't know how far away the test center is but the catalyst takes a few minutes to warm up to full op temp.<P>Here in DE we get the thing in the exhaust pipe but we don't have the roller yet. (I think some parts of MD have it.) If you let a car sit to long in line waiting for inspection the catalyst can cool to low to work even with the car running. I failed once for just that reason. drove over to the other center after work, retested and passed just fine.||||reg||1
Z||000008||daved||11-14-2000||10:39 PM||||Just for interest, my 86 GT just passed the Ontario emissions with flying colours, and it has the original Cat. and about 125,000kM.  I changed the plugs the night before (and reset the computer even though I had no codes...but thats another story).  Filled up with Sunoco gas,  regular that has Ethanol in it, the theory being that it burns cleaner.<BR>           limit   reading<BR>HC ppm      76       65<BR>CO%        0.42     0.21<BR>NO ppm     848      488<BR>           rpm 2038<BR>They even test the gas cap!  <BR>So there is hope for these engines.  GM didn't do too bad a job if a 14 year old car still passes emissions!||||reg||1
Z||000009||Raydar||11-14-2000||10:58 PM||||My 88 Duck failed for NOX. Replaced the EGR valve. Passed just fine. <BR>Just for grins, try to fill up with premium before you get it tested. Couldn't hurt.<P>Raydar <BR> ||||reg||1
Z||000010||JohnnyK||11-14-2000||11:33 PM||||daved: We have to do emission testing?? I didn't have to do that when I got it certified back in the summer...||||reg||1
Z||000011||Vlad||11-15-2000||12:35 AM||||Try this...<P>Once you have less then half tank of gas, get 1 gallon canister of Methil Alcohol from any autopart store and go to gas station. Put about 1.5 - 2.0 litre of Methil Alcohol <BR>into the tank and fill it up with premium gas. Drive at least 10 miles on highway, carefully redline it sevral times then, and then when car is stil hot go for test.<BR>Assuming that cat. converter is OK and O2 sensor isn't bad, you should pass even without EGR. <BR>At least this worked for me and couple other people. <BR>You will be surprised how the car drives. It feels like at least 20-30 more ponies. However, don't use alcohol often or you will burn pistons.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000012||Standard||11-15-2000||01:29 AM||||One of my friend's dad always buys these old junk cars and drives them until they die, and after he fails the testing the first time he goes and gets one of those additives that guarantees you'll pass emissions and it's worked every time for him!  <P>------------------<BR>Mike -<BR>Black 86.5 GT 5 Spd, 151,000 miles and counting!         <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/BURNOUT.gif">      <BR>Red 84 Sport Coupe - winter donut maker   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/asthanos.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000013||stsmithgt||11-15-2000||10:24 AM||||My 86GT failed smog by having the timing too far advanced.  When it was set correctly, it passed all tests at about half the average reading. SSOOOOOOOOOoooo clean. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000014||Raydar||11-15-2000||01:58 PM||||Vlad,<BR>Seems like I heard that methyl alcohol would damage fuel system components, unless they are of a specific design. (Maybe that was just Holley carb parts.)  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><BR>Anybody?<P>Raydar||||reg||1
Z||000015||Vlad||11-15-2000||03:31 PM||||Ok...... Lets see what it can damaged..<P>First of all, gas that we buy now already has significant amount of alcohol in it. So it isn't peally poison  for engine.<P>Imaging that you have a full tank of alcohol only. Don't do it..... Just think..... )))<P>Can it damage the tank and metal fuel lines...... I don't think so. <BR>Can it damage rubber parts? Definately, but not from the short time contact with deluted liquid suspention.<BR>What else? Alluminium fuel rails? Not really..<BR>Injectors...... Again, not from short time impact. <P>The higher concentration of alcohole in gas rises the temprature of combustion. Thus, it burns all that stuff that otherwise would be detected in exhaust.<BR>After doing this in several cars for a couple of years (not Fiero only) I havn't notised any bad side effect.<P>But that's me. Anybody else?||||reg||1
Z||000016||Scott_M||11-17-2000||12:15 AM||||Thanks for all the ideas. I think right now I'm gonna just replace the plugs clean up the EGR valve and make sure the car is warmed up when I take it in. Last time I passed with flying colors, and since then I've replaced nearly all the sensors and filters, so I don't know what else it could be. It's just more ammo for my fiance who wants me to sell it anyway. She just can't understand why I like the car so much. It's "Bizarre " she says, how SOoooo many people are so "into" that car(sigh). Oh well...Come to think of it, I can't understand how someone couldn't be "into" the car. HA! ||||reg||1
Z||000017||Scott_M||12-07-2000||09:46 PM||||Well after changing the plugs, an oil change, adding a "Guaranteed to pass" (G2P) gas additive, and reseatting the EGR valve, the old girl STILL didn't pass! In fact she did worse.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> Since it's the NOx test that was failed both times, is there a way of determining if the EGR valve is wasted? Without having to go to a shop to do it? I had to go to a "test only" center, so no repairs could even be attempted. $36.75 WASTED!  Thank God California has a new "retirement"program where they give you $1000 for your car if you can't afford to fight with it anymore. Sorry to vent...I just really thought it would pass, and I'm feeling just alittle betrayed....I know..I need help...the psych kind. I ffel like I'm already going through withdrawls..hehe<BR>||||reg||8
Z||000018||bubbajoexx||12-07-2000||10:26 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by JohnnyK:<BR><B>daved: We have to do emission testing?? I didn't have to do that when I got it certified back in the summer...</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>emission testing in ontario is for all cars and truck  registerd south of highway 9 easy to windsor east to kingston nothing north of highway 9||||reg||1
Z||000019||bubbajoexx||12-07-2000||10:33 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Scott_M:<BR><B>Well after changing the plugs, an oil change, adding a "Guaranteed to pass" (G2P) gas additive, and reseatting the EGR valve, the old girl STILL didn't pass! In fact she did worse.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> Since it's the NOx test that was failed both times, is there a way of determining if the EGR valve is wasted? Without having to go to a shop to do it? I had to go to a "test only" center, so no repairs could even be attempted. $36.75 WASTED!  Thank God California has a new "retirement"program where they give you $1000 for your car if you can't afford to fight with it anymore. Sorry to vent...I just really thought it would pass, and I'm feeling just alittle betrayed....I know..I need help...the psych kind. I ffel like I'm already going through withdrawls..hehe</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>mmost of the time when you get a high NOX reading the EGR needs cleaning and you need to change the catalitic converter the bead type converters dont like sitting for a long time chang it to a daul bed monolith converter and you should have no more problems.JOE||||reg||10
